    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented `riskFlag` on the owner create/read response so it's `true` when **any** of the three signals hold, else `false`.\n\n**How it works** \u2014 all three inputs are already persisted on the `Owner` entity (`possibleDuplicate`, `capacityWarning`, and `email` columns), so `riskFlag` is a *derived* field computed in the MapStruct mapper. That makes it identical whether returned from `POST /api/owners` or read back later via `GET /api/owners/{id}`, with no new pipeline step or DB column needed.\n\nChanges:\n1. **`RiskFlag.java`** (new helper in `function/owner`, matching the style of `OwnerSegment`/`Locality`): `RiskFlag.of(owner)` returns `possibleDuplicate || capacityWarning || disposable-adjacent email`.\n   - *possible duplicate* \u2192 `owner.isPossibleDuplicate()` (soft lastName+postcode match).\n   - *city over soft capacity* \u2192 `owner.isCapacityWarning()` (city held 40\u201349, approaching the hard limit of 50).\n   - *disposable-adjacent email* \u2192 a new concept: the domain isn't itself on the disposable blocklist (those are rejected with 400 upstream and never reach a created owner), but shares the registrable label of one \u2014 a subdomain (`x.mailinator.com`) or the same second-level name under another TLD (`mailinator.net`). Absent/domainless email is not adjacent.\n2. **`CheckOwnerEmailDomain.java`** \u2014 widened `DISPOSABLE_DOMAINS` to package-private so `RiskFlag` derives the adjacency labels from the single source of truth.\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added a `riskFlag` mapping expression.\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `riskFlag` read-only boolean schema field (regenerates the `OwnerDto`).\n\nVerification: the existing `AcceptanceTest.coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` (a normal, email-less structured owner \u21d2 `riskFlag` false) passes, and the full suite is **BUILD SUCCESS**. I confirmed the generated `OwnerDto` and `OwnerMapperImpl` wire the field through.\n\nNote: only the false-path case is covered by a test; the disposable-adjacent definition is my faithful reading of the one-line spec (no existing definition existed in the repo), documented in the code and OpenAPI description.",
